these people endure a level of suffering  that is difficult to imagine  most old and many have emotional or  psychological problems  their families live far away or fled the  fighting months ago  they Brave the shelling to come here for  soup and to charge their phones  Russia's bombardment of Bahamut is  getting heavier by the day  my neighbor was killed in her Cellar she  burned alive I fled my home four days  ago during the fighting I had to crawl  along the street to escape there was an  explosion just meters away from me  to destroyed Central town is almost  completely deserted Russian forces are  only around a kilometer away the  artillery explosions are relentless  some people wait patiently in the cold  whisking their lives desperate for food  and humanitarian Aid  others are so hungry they seem oblivious  to the danger of Simply standing outside  I cannot get all the aid I need without  documents and they all burned when my  house was hit on the other side of the  river  many of the people here on the western  side of town are going to food  distribution centers and these makeshift  shops we understand that the majority of  the heaviest fighting is the north the  East and increasingly the southeast of  the city  but of course there is nowhere safe in  Bahamut  most of the people who lived in these  Flats have fled  retired engineer Victor Casino leads us  upstairs  he and his wife Galena and mother-in-law  are the only people left in their  apartment block  93 year old Maria lies wrapped under  blankets in the dark  frail and afraid she wants to see her  son but he lives across the front line  in russian-occupied territory  my life was good before the war she says  we built a nice house we had everything  but then the fighting started  what  picture shows us where they store rain  water for drinking there's been no water  or electricity in bakward for months  he worked in the oil industry for 25  years before the breakup of the Soviet  Union and so is entitled to a Russian  pension  I know if there was a corridor to where  my brother-in-law lives then we would  try to leave or if we could get to  Moscow or St Petersburg where our sons  live but we don't have any money because  we can't get our Russian pensions  anymore  not  most people stopped living above ground  months ago  lude Miller and five other people are  Sheltering in this basement  the shelling has got much worse in the  last couple of days I'm too afraid to  leave the cellar I Preston promised that  he would liberate donbass by new year  but it never happened  life before the war is nothing but a  memory for these people  surviving the nightmare inflicted on  them is all they can hope for now  Charles Stratford Al Jazeera
 

